{
  "id": "SPEC-GITHUB-RELEASE-HARDENING-003",
  "status": "proposed",
  "owner_track": "release-security",
  "last_verified_revision": "59023bd3286650394af37bc34bd688b51dba3d92",
  "depends_on": [
    "SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001A",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001B",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001C",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-002"
  ],
  "purpose": "Harden the GitHub Actions release adapter so registry publication and repository mutation occur only through a narrowly authorized, reviewable release boundary.",
  "scope": ".github/workflows/callisto-release.yml and its referenced actions, GitHub Environment configuration documentation, action pinning, permissions, artifact handoff, and workflow tests. The non_goal below of not making GitHub Actions the only supported CI/release platform refers to the portability of the callisto CLI as a build tool; it does not constrain the mechanisms this repository's own meta-release pipeline may use. A GitHub-Environment-specific protection gate on this repo's release workflow (AC-001) is in scope and does not conflict with that non-goal. This spec declares its build dependencies explicitly in depends_on. SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001A defines the durable-plan envelope schema; SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001B defines the CLI command-mode separation and operation-status record; SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001C owns the plan-job to execute-job topology and the artifact upload/download flow. None of the three exists in the current workflow, where a single `release` job invokes the composite callisto-action and computes the plan inline (`callisto plan-publish`) in the same shell block that pushes tags and creates the GitHub release. AC-001 and AC-006 consume that topology and that envelope rather than defining them, and must therefore be sequenced after all three ship. SPEC-RELEASE-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-002 owns source_commit resolution and the structured HEAD-vs-source_commit mismatch error that AC-006's validation step matches on.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Making GitHub Actions the only supported CI or release platform.",
    "Moving provider-neutral release rules from callisto core into workflow shell code.",
    "Requiring Git tag signing in this track; signature policy is covered by SPEC-RELEASE-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-002.",
    "Defining the plan/execute job topology or the durable-plan envelope schema. Those are owned by SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001A/001B/001C; this spec consumes them and adds the authorization, pinning, permission, and review boundary around the execute job."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "The workflow's current single `release` job (which today computes the plan and performs tag/publish/gh-release in one shell script) is split into two jobs per the plan-job to execute-job topology defined by SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001C: an unprivileged planning job with no registry secrets and a read-only or no GITHUB_TOKEN, and a separate privileged execution job that performs tag/publish/release actions. The execution job is bound to a GitHub Environment (`environment:` key referencing a named environment such as `release`) that has required reviewers and branch/tag deployment rules configured. The repository documents that environment's name, its required reviewers, its branch/tag deployment rules, and an administrator-verifiable API or console procedure for confirming those rules are actually configured on the repository. The workflow YAML `environment:` reference alone is explicitly insufficient evidence that protection rules exist: the key declares intent, and GitHub auto-creates a referenced environment with zero protection rules on first use unless required reviewers have been separately configured via repo Settings. This criterion owns the topology and the documented procedure; AC-004 owns the automated enforcement that the documented rules are live. Non-release jobs (verify, planning) receive neither registry secrets nor write-scoped GITHUB_TOKEN permissions.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Each workflow and job declares the least GitHub token permissions it needs. contents, pull-requests, checks, and statuses are write-scoped only in the job that demonstrably performs that operation; all other jobs default to read or none. A workflow test or policy check rejects an unscoped top-level write grant.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Third-party GitHub Actions are pinned to immutable full commit SHAs (40 hex characters), each with a nearby comment recording the upstream tag or release used, for human review.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004b",
      "criterion": "CI verifies every external action reference's pin format and fails the build if any external action reference is changed to a mutable tag or branch instead of a full commit SHA.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "A CI or setup check calls the GitHub API (e.g. `gh api repos/{repo}/environments/{name}`) against the environment named in AC-001's `environment:` key and fails unless that environment's `protection_rules` array contains an entry with `\"type\": \"required_reviewers\"` and a non-empty `reviewers` list. A non-empty `protection_rules` array is not by itself sufficient: live GitHub data confirms environments commonly carry only a `branch_policy` rule (restricts which branch may deploy) with no human approval gate at all -- that shape must still fail this check. This check is the automated enforcement of AC-001's evidentiary rule, and it must read live environment configuration through the API rather than inspecting workflow YAML, so that a diff which merely adds `environment: release` can never by itself satisfy it.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "The planning job (per AC-001) uploads the explicit durable plan defined by SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001A as a build artifact per SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001C's upload/download flow. The privileged execution job downloads only that artifact from its own workflow run, verifies its source_commit against its own checkout per SPEC-RELEASE-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-002 AC-002, and fails before any registry or repository credentials are used if validation fails, surfacing that spec's structured mismatch error rather than a free-text-only diagnostic. Caches may accelerate dependency installation but are never read as release intent. Implementation of this criterion is blocked on SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001A/001B/001C shipping the envelope schema, command-mode separation, and artifact upload/download flow first; see depends_on and the scope note on sequencing.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "Changes to files under `.github/workflows/` and `.github/actions/` require review from a designated set of owners before merge, enforced via CODEOWNERS combined with a branch protection rule requiring that review on those paths. This is distinct from and in addition to AC-001's environment gate: the environment gate controls who can trigger a *deployment* from an already-merged workflow, while this criterion controls who can *merge changes* to the workflow/action definitions that determine what a future deployment does.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "AC-004b (SHA-pin drift detection): consider adopting the maintained `step-security/github-actions-ensure-sha-pinned-actions` GitHub Action rather than hand-rolling the check -- it is the actively maintained successor to the now-stale `zgosalvez/github-actions-ensure-sha-pinned-actions`. Whichever is adopted must itself be pinned to a commit SHA, or it reintroduces the exact circular-trust problem AC-003 targets.",
    "AC-003/AC-004b/AC-004 are pure GitHub Actions YAML/CLI concerns -- stay with `gh api` and shell, consistent with callisto-action's existing pattern (action.yml already shells to `gh` for every GitHub interaction). No Rust crate (e.g. octocrab) is warranted for this narrow, workflow-scoped check."
  ]
}
{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-INTERFACES-013",
  "status": "draft",
  "owner_track": "release-security",
  "last_verified_revision": "289001ec",
  "depends_on": [
    "SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-012"
  ],
  "purpose": "Expose the durable release architecture through a clear CLI and portable CI contract, and migrate Callisto's GitHub Actions release workflow to an artifact-bound, Environment-gated execution job without preserving legacy mutation bypasses.",
  "scope": "The release CLI command family, legacy-command transition, user-facing state-path and artifact inputs, GitHub Actions plan/build/execute topology, workflow permissions and action pinning, and documentation needed to operate the new flow. Provider-neutral planning, capability validation, state, and effects are owned by predecessor specs and consumed here.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Making GitHub Actions the only supported CI provider or moving core release authority into workflow shell scripts.",
    "Allowing a command-line flag, environment variable, CI cache, inline JSON, or workflow input to bypass release intent validation, Git trust, artifact verification, or ApplyPermit.",
    "Keeping legacy publish/tag mutation behavior indefinitely."
  ],
  "command_contract": {
    "release plan": "Read-only. From repeated exact qualified package selections or one named documented configuration-selection mode, computes one ReleaseDecisionV1 and embeds it in the durable ReleaseIntentV1 it writes from a clean detached checkout; it neither publishes, tags, pushes, nor creates a forge release.",
    "release inspect": "Read-only. Displays an intent, artifact manifest, state, or receipt with their binding and readiness checks; it does not recompute an unapproved intent as a side effect.",
    "release execute": "The sole production mutation entry point. It consumes explicit intent and, when needed, artifact-manifest inputs, validates them, obtains a fresh ValidatedReleaseIntent and ApplyPermit, and runs the coordinator from SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-012.",
    "release reconcile": "Read-only. Consumes explicit intent/state inputs and performs exact provider observations to report recoverability; it never publishes, tags, pushes, or creates a forge release."
  },
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Callisto exposes `release plan`, `release inspect`, `release execute`, and `release reconcile` as explicitly named commands or an equally explicit namespaced interface with the four contracts above. `release plan` requires repeated exact qualified package selections or one named documented configuration-selection mode and embeds the resulting decision in the intent. `release execute` requires an explicit durable intent file path; it never searches the checkout, falls back to a default plan, accepts an inline JSON authority string, accepts a new selection, or recomputes a plan after validation fails. It accepts an explicit artifact-manifest path when the intent declares binary slots, and an optional explicit state path; omitting the latter uses SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-012's outside-checkout default. Parse, trust, binding, capability, artifact, attestation, or state validation failures exit non-zero before credentials or mutating adapters are reached.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "`release plan`, `release inspect`, and `release reconcile` are structurally read-only: their reachable command paths cannot call registry publish, Git tag/push, forge-release creation, or any provider effect adapter. `release execute` is structurally the only production CLI route that can acquire an ApplyPermit and dispatch those adapters. Integration tests invoke every command against recording adapters and assert the exact allowed effect set, including that each error path dispatches none.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "During the documented migration window, `plan-publish`, `publish`, `tag --plan`, and `filter-plan` remain available only as clearly labelled read-only compatibility previews. They may render proposed operations but cannot publish, tag, push, write release state, create a forge release, or acquire ApplyPermit. Their help and diagnostics name `release plan`, `release execute`, or `release reconcile` as the replacement as appropriate. A separately documented breaking release removes the legacy commands rather than silently restoring their mutation behavior.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "The repository GitHub release workflow has separate plan, build, and execute jobs. The unprivileged plan job checks out the exact candidate commit, runs only read-only planning, and uploads the intent file plus its SHA-256 as a workflow artifact. The build job consumes that exact intent, builds the declared binary slots from the matching source commit, verifies its own intent digest, creates a GitHub artifact attestation for each binary with `attestations: write` and `id-token: write` only in that job, and uploads the artifact manifest and binaries as workflow artifacts. The privileged execute job downloads only artifacts from the same workflow run, independently verifies the intent SHA-256, source commit, artifact-manifest bindings, downloaded binary digests, and each binary's GitHub attestation against the configured repository and immutable signer workflow before invoking `release execute`. A plan/build/execute/attestation mismatch fails before registry, Git, or forge credentials are available.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "The GitHub execute job is the only job with release secrets or write-scoped token permissions and is bound to a named GitHub Environment with required reviewers and branch/tag deployment rules. Workflow documentation states the environment name and an administrator-verifiable procedure to confirm its live protection rules; a CI/setup check queries GitHub's API and rejects an environment without non-empty required reviewers. Workflow YAML alone is not evidence that the gate is configured. Planning/build jobs use read-only or no token permissions and receive neither registry nor forge credentials.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "Every workflow/job declares least-privilege permissions. Third-party GitHub Actions are pinned to immutable full commit SHAs with nearby upstream-release comments, and an automated policy check rejects mutable action tags or branches. CODEOWNERS plus branch protection requires designated review for changes under `.github/workflows/` and `.github/actions/`. Caches may accelerate dependency installation but cannot carry an intent, state, receipt, artifact manifest, artifact bytes, or authority across jobs/runs.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "The CLI reference and release-operation documentation provide one end-to-end local and CI recovery flow: create intent from a clean detached checkout; inspect it; build/produce the intent-bound artifact manifest when binaries are declared; execute with an optional explicit state path; reconcile after interruption. Documentation distinguishes source crate publishes from compiled binary uploads, explains that local default state is outside the checkout, and never instructs users to commit state or use GitHub cache for release authority/recovery.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "The composite action may be retained for non-privileged setup or read-only operations, but it cannot by itself enforce the plan/build/Environment-gated job boundary. The repository workflow must express that boundary.",
    "The artifact handoff is transport, not approval. The execute job repeats cryptographic and semantic binding checks in the Callisto CLI before adapter construction. GitHub artifact attestations prove build provenance for the binary bytes; the intent and manifest additionally prove those bytes are the assets this release approved.",
    "Other CI providers can implement the same explicit-file contract: immutable intent digest, exact source checkout, manifest-plus-byte verification, state artifact, and a separately protected execute credential boundary."
  ],
  "revision_note": "Split from umbrella SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-FOUNDATION-007 to keep user/CI entry points and GitHub-specific controls dependent on a settled provider-neutral execution contract."
}
